// @NAME: Getting Started
// @TITLE: Escaping text so it is safe to put inside HTML.
//
// `html` is one function. It escapes the five characters that can break out of
// an HTML element body or an attribute value, so text you did not write can be
// interpolated into markup without becoming markup.
//
// It is not a markdown renderer — that is `markdown`, which turns a document
// into HTML. It is not a sanitiser either: it does not filter markup or strip
// scripts. It makes attacker-supplied TEXT safe to place in markup, which is a
// different and much stronger guarantee than making attacker-supplied MARKUP
// safe. If you need to accept a subset of HTML from a user, this is not it.
//
// ## The whole library
//
// `escape_html` is a method on `text`, so it reads in the direction the data
// flows:
use html;

fn main() {
  raw = "Tom & Jerry's <b>\"show\"</b>";
  println("<p>{raw.escape_html()}</p>");

  // Each section below is a function and `main` calls it — a section `main`
  // does not call still compiles, and silently never runs its assertions.
  the_five();
  where_to_use_it();
  the_apostrophe();
}

// That prints:
//
//     <p>Tom &amp; Jerry&#x27;s &lt;b&gt;&quot;show&quot;&lt;/b&gt;</p>
//
// ## The five characters
//
// `&` becomes `&amp;`, `<` becomes `&lt;`, `>` becomes `&gt;`, `"` becomes
// `&quot;` and `'` becomes `&#x27;`.
//
// `&` is escaped first, so the entities the others produce are never escaped a
// second time — an ampersand-last implementation turns `<` into `&amp;lt;` and
// the reader sees the entity rather than the character.

fn the_five() {
  assert("&".escape_html() == "&amp;", "ampersand");
  assert("<".escape_html() == "&lt;", "less-than");
  assert(">".escape_html() == "&gt;", "greater-than");
  assert("\"".escape_html() == "&quot;", "double quote");
  assert("'".escape_html() == "&#x27;", "apostrophe");

  // The ordering property, stated as a test: one pass, no double-escaping.
  assert("<a>".escape_html() == "&lt;a&gt;", "no entity is escaped twice");

  // Text that needs nothing comes back unchanged.
  assert("plain text".escape_html() == "plain text", "untouched when it can be");
}

// ## Where it belongs
//
// Every place text you did not write reaches HTML output. That is the element
// body AND the attribute value, in either quoting style — a `<p>` needs it as
// much as an `<a title='...'>` does, which is what the apostrophe is for.

fn where_to_use_it() {
  wu_name = "O'Brien & Sons";
  wu_safe = wu_name.escape_html();
  wu_body = "<p>{wu_safe}</p>";
  wu_attr = "<a title='{wu_safe}'>x</a>";

  assert(!wu_body.contains("&amp;amp;"), "the body is escaped once, not twice");
  // The property that matters for the attribute: no raw apostrophe survives, so
  // nothing in the value can close the quote the markup opened.
  assert(!wu_safe.contains("'"), "no raw apostrophe reaches the attribute value");
  assert(wu_attr.contains("&#x27;"), "it is an entity instead");
}

// It does not escape for other contexts. Text going into a URL, a JavaScript
// string or a CSS value needs that context's own escaping; HTML entities
// protect none of them.
//
// ## The one that surprises people
//
// The `markdown` package exports `html_escape`, which looks like this function
// with its words swapped. It is not quite: `markdown::html_escape` escapes four
// characters and leaves the apostrophe alone.
//
// That is fine for the element bodies the renderer puts text into, and unsafe
// in a single-quoted attribute value. When you are writing markup yourself,
// reach for this package.

fn the_apostrophe() {
  ap_raw = "Jerry's";
  assert(ap_raw.escape_html() == "Jerry&#x27;s", "html escapes it");
}

// ## Where to go next
//
// There is nothing else here — one function, `#pure`, no dependencies and no
// state, so it behaves identically on the interpreter, `--native`, wasm and in
// the browser. `tests/01-escape.loft` in the package is the fuller set of cases.
